<h2 id="algebra-section-ascent" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="05A2">10.94</span> Ascending properties of modules</h2>

<p>
All of the properties of a module in Theorem <a href="/tag/059Z" data-tag="059Z">10.93.3</a> ascend along arbitrary ring maps: 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="05A3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05A3">Lemma <span data-tag="05A3">10.94.1</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $M$ be an $R$-module. Then: </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $M$ is flat, then the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is flat. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $M$ is Mittag-Leffler, then the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is Mittag-Leffler. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $M$ is a direct sum of countably generated $R$-modules, then the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is a direct sum of countably generated $S$-modules. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $M$ is projective, then the $S$-module $M \otimes _ R S$ is projective. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      All are obvious except (2). For this, use formulation (3) of being Mittag-Leffler from Proposition <a href="/tag/059E" data-tag="059E">10.88.6</a> and the fact that tensoring commutes with taking colimits. Alternatively, one can use the characterization of Proposition <a href="/tag/059M" data-tag="059M">10.89.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
